Make DYNAMICALLY LINKED i386 guests load: emulate sub-host-page mmap on the 32-bit guest path too.

Before this patch a dynamic i386 guest dies in its own dynamic linker:

    /…/getconf: error while loading shared libraries: libc.so.6: failed to map segment from shared object

The x86_64 sibling of the same binary runs fine on the same build, so the problem is 32-bit-specific.
The rejected call, from `strace -f -e trace=mmap`:

    mmap(0xf78a2000, 540672, PROT_READ, MAP_PRIVATE|MAP_FIXED|MAP_DENYWRITE, 4, 0x1b2000) = -1 EINVAL

Address and file offset are both 4 KiB- but not 16 KiB-aligned, which is normal and unavoidable for an x86
guest: an ELF only guarantees `vaddr == offset (mod 4096)`, and `mmap2` takes its offset in 4096-byte
units. patches/0001 already emulates exactly this — but only on the path a 64-bit guest takes. GuestMmap
splits first:

    bool Map32Bit = !Is64Bit || (flags & FEX::HLE::X86_64_MAP_32BIT);
    if (Map32Bit) {
      Result = (uint64_t)Get32BitAllocator()->Mmap(addr, length, prot, flags, fd, offset);
    } else {
      Result = ::mmap(...);      // <- where 0001 put the emulation
    }

so every 32-bit guest mapping goes through `Get32BitAllocator()` instead. Which allocator that is takes
one more step to pin down, and it is not the obvious one: `Get32BitAllocator()` is virtual, and
`x32SyscallHandler` overrides it to return the allocator it was constructed with — which `InitAllocator()`
builds as a PASSTHROUGH allocator, because a 64-bit host kernel can place low-4GB mappings itself and
needs no arbitration. So under FEXInterpreter the live path is `MemAllocatorPassThrough::Mmap`, a bare
`::mmap`, and that is what produced the trace above.

Fixed in three places, all of them the same one-line substitution of MapExact for `::mmap`:

  * `MemAllocatorPassThrough::Mmap` — THE LIVE PATH for a 32-bit guest on a 64-bit host.
  * `MemAllocator32Bit::Mmap` — the sibling reached via the base handler's `Alloc32Handler` (a 32-bit
    host, and the TestHarnessRunner). Carries the identical bug; fixed rather than left as a trap.
  * the 64-bit direct-mmap branch in GuestMmap, which keeps 0001's behaviour but now shares the code.

`MemAllocator32Bit` has two 4 KiB-granular `::mmap` calls and both are addressed:

  * THE GUEST-CHOSEN ADDRESS (the `Addr != 0` path). The guest has already relocated against this address
    and it cannot be moved, so the mapping is emulated: back the enclosing host-page span with private
    anonymous memory and pread the file's bytes in.
  * THE FEX-CHOSEN ADDRESS (`AllocateNoHint`). Here the address is ours to pick, so pick one the kernel
    accepts — ask the free-page scan for enough slack to absorb host-page rounding, then round up. This
    also mattered more than it looks: that path maps with MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE and its retry logic only
    treats EEXIST as "try elsewhere", so a misaligned candidate returned EINVAL and failed the whole
    allocation rather than moving on.

The emulation itself moves into a new header, Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LargePageMap.h, and 0001's
inline copy is replaced by a call to it. One implementation, three call sites: a routine that rewrites
another process's mappings byte-for-byte is not something to maintain twice. Header-only, so no
CMakeLists change.

TWO DELIBERATE NARROWINGS, both of them about not answering a request with a different mapping:

  * ADDRESS. `MapExact` emulates only when MAP_FIXED is set AND MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE is not. Without
    MAP_FIXED the address is a hint the kernel may place elsewhere, and forcing it would silently unmap
    whatever is there; with NOREPLACE the caller is explicitly asking not to displace an existing mapping,
    which an emulated MAP_FIXED would do. Both go to the kernel unchanged — and a NOREPLACE caller that
    gets EINVAL can choose another address, which is precisely what the 32-bit allocator now does.
  * SEMANTICS. The emulation backs the span with PRIVATE ANONYMOUS memory, which reproduces a private
    file-backed or anonymous mapping and nothing else, so it is restricted to exactly that: the type bits
    must be MAP_PRIVATE (tested as a unit, since MAP_SHARED_VALIDATE is MAP_SHARED|MAP_PRIVATE), and no
    flag outside a small honoured set may be present. A MAP_SHARED mapping would otherwise come back
    without writeback, without cross-process visibility and unshared across fork(); MAP_GROWSDOWN would
    lose its auto-growth; MAP_HUGETLB/MAP_LOCKED/MAP_SYNC each promise what an anonymous span does not.
    Those all reach the kernel untouched and fail with EINVAL as on an unpatched FEX — which also keeps
    FEX's VMA bookkeeping, recording the flags the GUEST passed, true of the mapping that exists.
    Nothing on the ELF-loading path this patch exists for is affected: those mappings are MAP_PRIVATE.

On a 4 KiB host every change here is a no-op: the slack is zero, the rounding is identity, and MapExact's
alignment test never fires.

--- a/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LargePageMap.h
+++ b/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LargePageMap.h
@@ -0,0 +1,136 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
+#pragma once
+
+// Mapping a GUEST page onto a host whose page is larger than 4 KiB.
+//
+// An x86 guest's page is 4 KiB and its ABI says so: an ELF only guarantees `vaddr == offset (mod 4096)`,
+// and `mmap2` takes its file offset in 4096-byte units. So a guest — its dynamic linker above all —
+// routinely asks for a file-backed MAP_FIXED whose address and offset are 4 KiB-aligned but not
+// host-page-aligned. The kernel rejects that outright with EINVAL, which is why no dynamically linked
+// guest can load its libc on a 16 KiB host.
+//
+// `MapExact` honours those requests by emulating them: back the whole enclosing host-page span with
+// private anonymous memory and copy the file's bytes in with pread. Header-only and free of FEX
+// dependencies so that both the 64-bit direct-mmap path (SyscallsSMCTracking) and the 32-bit guest
+// allocator (LinuxAllocator) can share one implementation — the routine is subtle enough that two copies
+// would certainly drift.
+
+#include <cerrno>
+#include <cstdint>
+#include <cstdlib>
+#include <cstring>
+#include <fcntl.h>
+#include <sys/mman.h>
+#include <unistd.h>
+
+// Parsed explicitly rather than relying on the host headers, which is what FEX does elsewhere for this
+// flag: a build against older UAPI headers would otherwise silently lose the NOREPLACE distinction below.
+#ifndef MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE
+#define MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE 0x100000
+#endif
+
+namespace FEX::LargePage {
+
+inline size_t HostPageSize() {
+  static const size_t Size = [] {
+    const long Result = ::sysconf(_SC_PAGESIZE);
+    return Result > 0 ? static_cast<size_t>(Result) : static_cast<size_t>(4096);
+  }();
+  return Size;
+}
+
+// Would this request be rejected by the kernel purely for straddling host pages?
+inline bool IsSubHostPage(uintptr_t addr, off_t offset) {
+  const size_t Mask = HostPageSize() - 1;
+  return (addr & Mask) || (static_cast<uint64_t>(offset) & Mask);
+}
+
+// Map `length` bytes at EXACTLY `addr`, emulating the mapping when `addr`/`offset` straddle host pages.
+// Returns the mapped address, or MAP_FAILED with errno set. An already-host-aligned request, or one with
+// no fixed address, is handed to the kernel untouched.
+inline void* MapExact(void* addr, size_t length, int prot, int flags, int fd, off_t offset) {
+  const uintptr_t AddrU = reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(addr);
+  const bool FileBacked = fd >= 0 && !(flags & MAP_ANONYMOUS);
+  const bool AnonMap = (flags & MAP_ANONYMOUS) != 0;
+
+  // Emulate ONLY where the caller has demanded this exact address and is prepared for it to be taken.
+  // Without MAP_FIXED the address is merely a hint the kernel may ignore, and forcing it would silently
+  // unmap whatever lives there; with MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE the caller is explicitly asking NOT to displace
+  // an existing mapping, which an emulated MAP_FIXED would do. Both cases go to the kernel unchanged, and
+  // a NOREPLACE caller that gets EINVAL can pick another address — which is what FEX's 32-bit allocator
+  // does.
+  const bool ExactRequested = (flags & MAP_FIXED) && !(flags & MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE);
+
+  // AND ONLY WHERE THE RESULT IS THE MAPPING THAT WAS ASKED FOR. The span below is recreated as PRIVATE
+  // ANONYMOUS memory with the file's bytes read into it, which reproduces a private file-backed or
+  // anonymous mapping exactly — and reproduces nothing else. MAP_SHARED would lose writeback,
+  // cross-process visibility and post-fork sharing; MAP_GROWSDOWN would lose its auto-growth; MAP_HUGETLB,
+  // MAP_LOCKED and MAP_SYNC each promise something a plain anonymous span does not. Every such request
+  // goes to the kernel UNTOUCHED, where a sub-host-page address fails with EINVAL exactly as it does on an
+  // unpatched FEX: a refusal the caller can see and react to beats a mapping whose semantics silently
+  // differ from the requested ones — and it keeps FEX's own VMA bookkeeping, which records the flags the
+  // GUEST passed, true of the mapping that actually exists.
+  //
+  // MAP_SHARED_VALIDATE is (MAP_SHARED | MAP_PRIVATE), so the type bits are tested AS A UNIT; a plain
+  // `!(flags & MAP_SHARED)` would let it through. The extras listed are the ones a private anonymous span
+  // may drop safely: DENYWRITE/EXECUTABLE are ignored by Linux, NORESERVE/POPULATE/NONBLOCK are
+  // performance hints, and MAP_STACK has no effect on Linux.
+  constexpr int HonouredFlags = MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_ANONYMOUS | MAP_FIXED | MAP_DENYWRITE | MAP_EXECUTABLE |
+                                MAP_NORESERVE | MAP_POPULATE | MAP_NONBLOCK | MAP_STACK;
+  const bool Reproducible = (flags & (MAP_SHARED | MAP_PRIVATE)) == MAP_PRIVATE && !(flags & ~HonouredFlags);
+
+  if (!AddrU || !ExactRequested || !Reproducible || !IsSubHostPage(AddrU, FileBacked ? offset : 0) ||
+      !(FileBacked || AnonMap)) {
+    return ::mmap(addr, length, prot, flags, fd, offset);
+  }
+
+  const size_t HostPage = HostPageSize();
+  const uintptr_t SpanBase = AddrU & ~(HostPage - 1);
+  const uintptr_t SpanEnd = (AddrU + length + HostPage - 1) & ~(HostPage - 1);
+  const size_t LeadLen = AddrU - SpanBase;            // neighbour bytes sharing our leading host page
+  const size_t TrailLen = SpanEnd - (AddrU + length); // neighbour bytes sharing our trailing host page
+
+  // The end pages are shared with whatever the guest mapped next door, and replacing the span with fresh
+  // anonymous memory would erase it. Save those fragments first, through /proc/self/mem rather than a
+  // direct read: a neighbour page can itself be an unbacked beyond-EOF page, where a direct read raises
+  // SIGBUS but a pread merely comes up short.
+  static const int MemFd = ::open("/proc/self/mem", O_RDONLY | O_CLOEXEC);
+  uint8_t* LeadSave = LeadLen ? static_cast<uint8_t*>(::malloc(LeadLen)) : nullptr;
+  uint8_t* TrailSave = TrailLen ? static_cast<uint8_t*>(::malloc(TrailLen)) : nullptr;
+  const ssize_t LeadGot = (LeadSave && MemFd >= 0) ? ::pread(MemFd, LeadSave, LeadLen, SpanBase) : 0;
+  const ssize_t TrailGot = (TrailSave && MemFd >= 0) ? ::pread(MemFd, TrailSave, TrailLen, AddrU + length) : 0;
+
+  // R/W, and deliberately not the requested protection: FEX reads guest code in order to translate it and
+  // never executes a guest page on the host, so EXEC buys nothing, and W is needed to pread into the span.
+  // Sub-host-page protection is not enforceable here in any case.
+  void* Span = ::mmap(reinterpret_cast<void*>(SpanBase), SpanEnd - SpanBase, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE,
+                      MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_ANONYMOUS | MAP_FIXED, -1, 0);
+  bool Ok = Span != MAP_FAILED;
+
+  if (Ok) {
+    if (LeadGot > 0) {
+      ::memcpy(reinterpret_cast<void*>(SpanBase), LeadSave, static_cast<size_t>(LeadGot));
+    }
+    if (TrailGot > 0) {
+      ::memcpy(reinterpret_cast<void*>(AddrU + length), TrailSave, static_cast<size_t>(TrailGot));
+    }
+    if (FileBacked) {
+      // A short read is not an error: a segment's last page legitimately extends past end-of-file, and
+      // the bytes beyond it must read as zero, which the fresh anonymous mapping already provides.
+      Ok = ::pread(fd, reinterpret_cast<void*>(AddrU), length, offset) >= 0;
+    }
+    // An anonymous request needs nothing further — the new mapping is already zero-filled.
+  }
+
+  ::free(LeadSave);
+  ::free(TrailSave);
+
+  if (!Ok) {
+    // Leave errno from whatever failed and fall back to the kernel, so a caller that can cope with a
+    // refusal still sees one rather than a silently wrong mapping.
+    return ::mmap(addr, length, prot, flags, fd, offset);
+  }
+  return reinterpret_cast<void*>(AddrU);
+}
+
+} // namespace FEX::LargePage
